// Title: a whole capture inside a slice element is typed by the wrong member
// of the element union: `y@ `B(_)` gets type `A(i64).
// Command: timeout -s KILL 30 /home/eric/tmp/review-2026-10-26/bin/graphix --no-cache c-pattern-whole-capture-set-pairing.gx
//   (also --no-fusion; and `--check` with `let q: bool = y` in the arm prints
//   "bool does not contain `A(i64)")
// Expected: y is `B(string): the inner select is refused ("`A(n) will never
//   match `B(string)"), as it is when x is Array<[`A(i64), `B(string)]> with
//   no null.
// Observed: --check accepts. Both engines: "select y { `A(n) => n + 1 }: no
//   arm matches [.., "B", "s"]: a coverage hole in the check". With
//   `let `A(n) = y; n + 1` in the arm the node-walk binds n = "s" and logs an
//   arith error parsing "s" as an i64.
// PatternNode::wholes_by_member calls StructPatternNode::leaves with each raw
// scrutinee member and inferred = true; for the member
// Array<[`A(i64), `B(string)]>, child_types reads the element union as the
// slice's per-element Set (2 members = 1 element + rest) and pairs element 0
// with `A(i64). Needs a scrutinee of 2+ members (the null) and an element
// union whose member count equals the elements + rest.
let f = |x: [Array<[`A(i64), `B(string)]>, null]| select x {
  [y@ `B(_), ..] => select y { `A(n) => n + 1 },
  _ => 0
};
f([`B("s")])
